WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.662
A wide-area network carries traffic

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.620
between many pairs of sites.

3
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.661
The best routing comes from

4
00:00:06.724 --> 00:00:08.979
a linear program, which can be slow

5
00:00:08.979 --> 00:00:11.610
so operators use fast heuristics instead.

6
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.482
A heuristic is quick

7
00:00:13.482 --> 00:00:15.800
but sometimes far from optimal.

8
00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.194
MetaOpt, from NSDI 2024

9
00:00:18.194 --> 00:00:20.153
asks a sharp question

10
00:00:20.238 --> 00:00:22.367
on which traffic does the

11
00:00:22.452 --> 00:00:24.070
heuristic do worst?

12
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.220
The answer tells an operator

13
00:00:26.284 --> 00:00:28.020
what the shortcut can cost.

14
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.698
The heuristic in this task keeps

15
00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:32.600
the better of two shortcuts.

16
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.642
The first, called POP

17
00:00:34.642 --> 00:00:36.950
splits the pairs into groups.

18
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.662
Each group gets an equal share of every

19
00:00:39.723 --> 00:00:41.540
link and is solved on its own.

20
00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:43.717
The second, demand pinning

21
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:46.558
sends every small demand, of at most six

22
00:00:46.558 --> 00:00:48.790
straight down its shortest path

23
00:00:48.858 --> 00:00:50.685
and then optimizes the rest

24
00:00:50.752 --> 00:00:51.970
with what is left.

25
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.406
The gap is the optimal throughput

26
00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.240
minus the better of the two.

27
00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.270
Here is a small network of our own

28
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.070
four sites, five links, and five pairs

29
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.540
each with two paths.

30
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.385
Put a demand of six from A to C

31
00:01:06.463 --> 00:01:08.335
and eighteen each from A

32
00:01:08.413 --> 00:01:10.050
to B and from B to C.

33
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.720
The optimum carries thirty-six.

34
00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.447
With demand pinning

35
00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:16.720
the network carries twenty-four

36
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.510
and with POP, twenty-one.

37
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.700
So the gap is twelve.

38
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.080
Why does this traffic hurt both shortcuts?

39
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.115
Pinning puts the small demand from A to

40
00:01:27.184 --> 00:01:29.456
C on the links A to B and B to C

41
00:01:29.525 --> 00:01:31.246
exactly the two links the

42
00:01:31.315 --> 00:01:32.830
big demands need most.

43
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.564
The optimum would send it the other way

44
00:01:35.564 --> 00:01:36.220
through D.

45
00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:37.553
POP, meanwhile

46
00:01:37.553 --> 00:01:40.304
gives each group only half of every link

47
00:01:40.304 --> 00:01:43.252
so the two big demands, which share a group

48
00:01:43.252 --> 00:01:45.610
each get half of their direct link.

49
00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.994
With demand levels zero, six and eighteen

50
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.130
our five pairs give two hundred

51
00:01:51.197 --> 00:01:52.598
forty-three matrices

52
00:01:52.598 --> 00:01:54.201
so we can try them all

53
00:01:54.201 --> 00:01:56.270
and twelve is the largest gap.

54
00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.962
The real task has twenty-seven pairs

55
00:01:58.962 --> 00:02:00.862
at most nine of them nonzero

56
00:02:00.925 --> 00:02:03.204
and only two hundred fifty-six calls

57
00:02:03.268 --> 00:02:05.420
to the exact checker per instance.

58
00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.650
That is more than three billion matrices.

59
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.640
Trying them all is out of the question.

60
00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:14.525
The task removes MetaOpt's search

61
00:02:14.525 --> 00:02:16.220
and the agent writes it.

62
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.813
The authors' code solves one large

63
00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:20.330
mixed-integer program.

64
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.613
Claude Opus 5.5 wrote its own small simplex

65
00:02:23.681 --> 00:02:26.204
solver to score candidates privately

66
00:02:26.204 --> 00:02:29.000
then ran a local search on four threads.

67
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.869
Its own idea was to start

68
00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.205
the search from cuts

69
00:02:32.266 --> 00:02:33.967
small sets of links that the

70
00:02:34.028 --> 00:02:35.668
big demands must all cross

71
00:02:35.668 --> 00:02:37.794
crowded with small pinned demands

72
00:02:37.855 --> 00:02:39.313
just like the links from

73
00:02:39.374 --> 00:02:40.710
A to C in our example.

74
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.498
Opus scored fifty-two

75
00:02:42.563 --> 00:02:44.197
against forty-seven point

76
00:02:44.262 --> 00:02:45.700
eight for the authors.

77
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.600
Three other runs tied at fifty-two

78
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.130
and all four passed.

79
00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:53.270
So: the gap is the optimum minus

80
00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.950
the better shortcut.

81
00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.679
To find a large one

82
00:02:56.679 --> 00:02:59.300
crowd the links the big demands need.

83
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.676
And when exact checks are scarce

84
00:03:01.738 --> 00:03:03.518
score candidates yourself and

85
00:03:03.579 --> 00:03:04.930
spend the checks well.

86
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.255
As an exercise

87
00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.270
raise the capacity of the link

88
00:03:08.335 --> 00:03:09.765
from B to D to twelve

89
00:03:09.765 --> 00:03:12.560
and see whether the worst traffic changes.

90
00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.670
Next, Shockwave.
